# TODO: Steps for Implementing JWT Authentication & Authorization

#TODO: 1. Secure Endpoints with Role-Based Authorization
- [ ] Define roles (e.g., `USER`, `ADMIN`, etc.) for role-based access.
- [ ] Secure endpoints using `@PreAuthorize` or `@Secured` annotations.
    - Example:
      ```java
      @PreAuthorize("hasRole('ADMIN')")
      @GetMapping("/admin")
      public String getAdminData() {
          return "Admin Data";
      }
      ```

#TODO: 2. Handle JWT Expiration
- [ ] Implement expiration handling in JWT. Ensure token expiry is checked with a method like `isTokenExpired`.
    - Example:
      ```java
      private boolean isTokenExpired(String token) {
          return extractExpiration(token).before(new Date());
      }
      ```
- [ ] Return HTTP status `401 Unauthorized` if the token has expired.

#TODO:  3. Implement Refresh Token Mechanism
- [ ] Create a refresh token endpoint (e.g., `/refresh-token`) to allow users to obtain a new JWT after token expiration.
    - Example:
      ```java
      @PostMapping("/refresh-token")
      public ResponseEntity<?> refreshToken(@RequestBody String refreshToken) {
          if (jwtService.isTokenValid(refreshToken)) {
              String newToken = jwtService.generateToken(userDetails);
              return ResponseEntity.ok(new AuthenticationResponse(newToken));
          }
          return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.UNAUTHORIZED).body("Invalid refresh token");
      }
      ```

#TODO:  4. Exception Handling for Unauthorized Access
- [ ] Implement global exception handling for `Unauthorized` access.
    - Example:
      ```java
      @ControllerAdvice
      public class GlobalExceptionHandler {
          @ExceptionHandler(AccessDeniedException.class)
          public ResponseEntity<String> handleAccessDeniedException(AccessDeniedException ex) {
              return new ResponseEntity<>("Access denied", HttpStatus.FORBIDDEN);
          }
      }
      ```

#TODO:  5. JWT Filter for Validating the Token
- [ ] Implement a `JwtAuthenticationFilter` to validate JWT in every request and set the security context.
    - Example:
      ```java
      @Component
      public class JwtAuthenticationFilter extends OncePerRequestFilter {
          // Filter implementation
      }
      ```

#TODO:  6. Logging and Auditing
- [ ] Log sensitive information carefully (avoid logging JWTs or passwords).
- [ ] Implement auditing to track who accessed what and when, especially for sensitive operations.

#TODO:  7. Secure JWT Secrets
- [ ] Store `SECRET_KEY` in environment variables or a secret management tool (not hardcoded).
- [ ] Ensure the signing key is strong and complex.

#TODO:  8. CORS Configuration (Cross-Origin Resource Sharing)
- [ ] Configure CORS properly if the frontend and backend are on different domains.
    - Example:
      ```java
      @Configuration
      public class WebConfig implements WebMvcConfigurer {
          @Override
          public void addCorsMappings(CorsRegistry registry) {
              registry.addMapping("/**").allowedOrigins("http://localhost:3000").allowedMethods("GET", "POST", "PUT", "DELETE");
          }
      }
      ```

#TODO:  9. Secure JWT Transmission
- [ ] Ensure JWT is sent over HTTPS to avoid MITM (Man-in-the-Middle) attacks.
- [ ] Consider storing JWT in `HttpOnly` cookies for better security.

#TODO:  10. Logout Implementation
- [ ] Implement logout functionality to delete JWT on the client-side, especially if stored in local storage or cookies.
- [ ] Ensure proper invalidation of tokens upon logout.

#TODO:  11. Testing and Monitoring
- [ ] Write unit and integration tests for JWT authentication and authorization.
- [ ] Monitor JWT usage and check for invalid or expired token usage.

#TODO:  12. Update JWT Mechanism
- [ ] Implement token revocation or blacklisting if necessary.
- [ ] Consider adding custom claims (roles, permissions, etc.) to the JWT payload for more fine-grained authorization control.

#TODO:  Additional Notes:
- [ ] Review security best practices for JWT (e.g., short expiration time, secure storage, etc.).
- [ ] Ensure the user can only access resources permitted by their role and permissions.
